IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `xv-rules`

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) -- worked cases for every exported question in xv-rules.l4.
-- Expected values were worked by hand from the deposited text (section cited on each group), never copied from the
-- encoding's output. The edges: each limb of every "or" on and off, each "and" condition failing alone, the s 6A
-- exceptions against ss 5(1), 5(4) and 6, the s 6(9) appeal exception, and for the s 6(5)(b) notice the last day of the
-- directed period and the day after. One breach of the regulative rule is #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line (the stable
-- l4 cannot compare a breached trace with EQUALS).
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website, as collected by the Open
-- Australian Legal Corpus on 10 March 2025; may be out of date. State of New South Wales, CC BY 4.0.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `ss 3, 3A -- does the Act reach my case`

-- A negligence claim: civil, not under the Corporations Act. s 3(1).
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my case question` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: your case is a proceeding (a civil one) to which the Act applies (s 3(1))`
-- A prosecution: s 3(1) "proceeding does not include a criminal proceeding".
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my case question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act (s 3(1) "proceeding")`
-- A Corporations Act civil matter: s 3A.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my case question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Act does not apply to jurisdiction dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) (s 3A)`

§ `s 3 -- State matter`

-- s 3(1) limb (a), limb (b), both, neither.
#ASSERT `Is my matter a State matter question` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my matter a State matter question` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `Is my matter a State matter question` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Is my matter a State matter question` FALSE FALSE))

§ `s 4 -- a court of another State`

-- A NSW negligence claim in the Victorian Supreme Court: s 4(3).
#ASSERT `Can a court of another State hear my NSW matter question` `the Supreme Court of another State (including the NT and the ACT) or of a Territory` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has original and appellate jurisdiction in State matters (s 4(3))`
-- A matter removed under s 8 is a State matter by limb (b): s 4(4) for the Family Court of WA.
#ASSERT `Can a court of another State hear my NSW matter question` `the State Family Court of another State (the Family Court of Western Australia)` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the State Family Court of another State has original and appellate jurisdiction in State matters (s 4(4))`
-- A criminal matter: s 4(5), even though a State matter.
#ASSERT `Can a court of another State hear my NSW matter question` `the Supreme Court of another State (including the NT and the ACT) or of a Territory` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 4 vests no jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters (s 4(5))`
-- Not a State matter.
#ASSERT `Can a court of another State hear my NSW matter question` `the Supreme Court of another State (including the NT and the ACT) or of a Territory` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: your matter is not a State matter, and s 4 vests only State matters`
-- A District Court of another State: s 4 names only Supreme Courts and State Family Courts.
#ASSERT `Can a court of another State hear my NSW matter question` `another court` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 4 vests jurisdiction only in the Supreme Courts and State Family Courts of other States`

§ `s 5(1), s 6A(3) -- to a federal court`

-- The Supreme Court thinks the Federal Court more appropriate; no s 6A facts.
#ASSERT `Must my case go to a federal court question` `more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
#ASSERT `Must my case go to a federal court question` `more appropriate that the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia decide it` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (s 5(1))`
#ASSERT `Must my case go to a federal court question` `neither` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Supreme Court has not formed the view s 5(1) requires, so s 5(1) does not require a transfer`
-- All four s 6A(1) conditions: s 6A(3) lifts the duty even though the court prefers the Federal Court.
#ASSERT `Must my case go to a federal court question` `more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 6A(3) -- s 5(1) does not require the transfer, though the Supreme Court may transfer it if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
-- Each s 6A(1) condition failing alone: s 6A does not apply, so s 5(1) binds.
#ASSERT `Must my case go to a federal court question` `more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
#ASSERT `Must my case go to a federal court question` `more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
#ASSERT `Must my case go to a federal court question` `more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
#ASSERT `Must my case go to a federal court question` `more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`

§ `s 5(2) -- to another Supreme Court`

-- Limb (i): related case pending in Queensland and more appropriate there.
#ASSERT `Must my case go to another Supreme Court question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court, as it is related to a case pending there and more appropriately decided there (s 5(2)(b)(i))`
-- Limb (i) needs both: the court's view without a related case pending does not satisfy it.
#ASSERT `Must my case go to another Supreme Court question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the NSW Supreme Court has not formed any of the views s 5(2) requires, so s 5(2) does not require a transfer`
#ASSERT `Must my case go to another Supreme Court question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the NSW Supreme Court has not formed any of the views s 5(2) requires, so s 5(2) does not require a transfer`
-- Limb (ii) alone.
#ASSERT `Must my case go to another Supreme Court question` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court, as more appropriately decided there having regard to where it could have been brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(ii))`
-- Limb (iii) alone: a road accident in Victoria, witnesses there.
#ASSERT `Must my case go to another Supreme Court question` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(iii))`
#ASSERT `Must my case go to another Supreme Court question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the NSW Supreme Court has not formed any of the views s 5(2) requires, so s 5(2) does not require a transfer`

§ `s 5(6) -- the related case follows`

#ASSERT `Must my related case follow question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Must my related case follow question` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Must my related case follow question` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Must my related case follow question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Must my related case follow question` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE))

§ `s 5(7) -- who may apply`

#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer question` `a party (plaintiff, defendant or other party)` EQUALS `yes: a party to the proceeding may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7)); the court may also transfer of its own motion`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer question` `a person who has intervened in the case` EQUALS `yes: an intervener is a party (s 3(1) "party") and may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer question` `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory` EQUALS `yes: an Attorney-General may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer question` `someone else` EQUALS `not covered: s 5(7) names parties and Attorneys-General; the court may still transfer of its own motion`

§ `s 6 -- special federal matters`

-- Arguments: paragraph, s 6(9) appeal, s 6(3) order, whole within jurisdiction, then the other three s 6A(1) facts.
-- Paragraph (a), (b) or (d), no order, whole case within the Federal Court's jurisdiction: s 6(1), (2)(a).
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (a), (b) or (d) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer the whole proceeding to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (2)(a))`
-- Only part within the Federal Court's jurisdiction: s 6(1A).
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (a), (b) or (d) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer to the Federal Court only the part within the Federal Court's jurisdiction, including its accrued jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`
-- Paragraph (ab): s 6(2)(b), whole and part.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer the whole proceeding to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (2)(b))`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer only the part within the jurisdiction of whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(b))`
-- A s 6(3) order: s 6(1)(b) not met.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (a), (b) or (d) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(1)(b))`
-- An appeal where the trial court kept the matter: s 6(9).
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (a), (b) or (d) of the Commonwealth definition` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 6 does not apply to an appeal to the Full Court where the trial court made a s 6(3) order on this matter (s 6(9))`
-- s 6A(3): all four s 6A(1) facts.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 6A(3) -- s 6 does not require the transfer, though the Supreme Court may transfer it if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
-- Three of four s 6A(1) facts: s 6A does not apply, s 6 binds.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer the whole proceeding to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (2)(a))`
-- The other s 6A(1) facts without a paragraph (c) or (e) matter: s 6A(1)(a) fails, s 6 binds.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `paragraph (a), (b) or (d) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer the whole proceeding to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (2)(a))`
-- No special federal matter.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` `no special federal matter` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: your case raises no special federal matter, so s 6 does not require a transfer (s 6(1)(a))`

§ `s 6(3), (4) -- keeping a special federal matter`

#ASSERT `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court may order that it decide the case itself (s 6(3), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Supreme Court cannot keep it until written notice of the special federal matter has been given to both the Commonwealth and the NSW Attorneys-General (s 6(4)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Supreme Court cannot keep it until a reasonable time has passed for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions (s 6(4)(b))`
-- Only the parties' convenience: s 6(3) excludes it.
#ASSERT `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Supreme Court may keep it only for special reasons other than the parties' convenience (s 6(3))`

§ `s 6(4), (5)(b) -- the directed notice`

-- Direction on 2 March 2026 allowing 14 days: the last day is 16 March 2026 (day 14).
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2026) 14 TRUE (DATE_FROM_DMY 16 3 2026) (DATE_FROM_DMY 20 3 2026) EQUALS `yes: you gave the notice within the time the court's direction allowed`
-- Given on 17 March 2026 (day 15): late.
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2026) 14 TRUE (DATE_FROM_DMY 17 3 2026) (DATE_FROM_DMY 20 3 2026) EQUALS `no: you gave the notice, but after the time the court's direction allowed had ended`
-- Given the same day as the direction (day 0).
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2026) 14 TRUE (DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2026) (DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2026) EQUALS `yes: you gave the notice within the time the court's direction allowed`
-- Not given; today 16 March 2026, the last day: not yet due.
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2026) 14 FALSE (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2026) (DATE_FROM_DMY 16 3 2026) EQUALS `not yet due: you have not given the notice, but the time the direction allows has not ended`
-- Not given; today 17 March 2026: overdue.
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2026) 14 FALSE (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2026) (DATE_FROM_DMY 17 3 2026) EQUALS `overdue: the time the direction allows has ended and you have not given the notice; until it is given the court cannot keep the case under s 6(3)`
-- Across a month and year end: direction 20 December 2027, 21 days, last day 10 January 2028.
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 20 12 2027) 21 TRUE (DATE_FROM_DMY 10 1 2028) (DATE_FROM_DMY 12 1 2028) EQUALS `yes: you gave the notice within the time the court's direction allowed`
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 20 12 2027) 21 TRUE (DATE_FROM_DMY 11 1 2028) (DATE_FROM_DMY 12 1 2028) EQUALS `no: you gave the notice, but after the time the court's direction allowed had ended`
-- Across 29 February 2028: direction 20 February 2028, 10 days, last day 1 March 2028.
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 20 2 2028) 10 FALSE (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2028) (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 3 2028) EQUALS `not yet due: you have not given the notice, but the time the direction allows has not ended`
#ASSERT `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` (DATE_FROM_DMY 20 2 2028) 10 FALSE (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2028) (DATE_FROM_DMY 2 3 2028) EQUALS `overdue: the time the direction allows has ended and you have not given the notice; until it is given the court cannot keep the case under s 6(3)`

#TRACE `s 6(5)(b) -- the directed notice to the Attorneys-General` 14 AT 0 WITH
    (WAITUNTIL 20)
-- expect: BREACH BY `the party directed to give notice` BECAUSE "s 6(5)(b): ..."

§ `s 6(7) -- urgent interlocutory relief`

#ASSERT `Can I still get urgent interim relief question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: s 6 does not stop the Supreme Court granting you urgent interlocutory relief, if it is in the interests of justice (s 6(7))`
#ASSERT `Can I still get urgent interim relief question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 6(7) saves only urgent interlocutory relief that the court considers in the interests of justice`
#ASSERT `Can I still get urgent interim relief question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: s 6(7) saves only urgent interlocutory relief that the court considers in the interests of justice`

§ `ss 6(8), 7(7) -- decisions saved`

#ASSERT `Is the decision invalid question` `the Supreme Court decided my special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice` TRUE EQUALS `no: s 6 does not invalidate a decision made through inadvertence (s 6(8))`
#ASSERT `Is the decision invalid question` `the Supreme Court decided my special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice` FALSE EQUALS `not covered: ss 6(8) and 7(7) save only the decisions they describe; the Act does not say what follows otherwise`
-- s 7(7)(a) saves a decision made under s 7(6) whether or not inadvertent.
#ASSERT `Is the decision invalid question` `the Full Court decided my appeal that should have gone to a federal Full Court, choosing to proceed in the interests of justice under s 7(6)` FALSE EQUALS `no: s 7 does not invalidate the Full Court's decision (s 7(7)(a))`
#ASSERT `Is the decision invalid question` `the Full Court decided my appeal that should have gone to a federal Full Court` TRUE EQUALS `no: s 7 does not invalidate a decision the Full Court made through inadvertence (s 7(7)(b))`
#ASSERT `Is the decision invalid question` `the Full Court decided my appeal that should have gone to a federal Full Court` FALSE EQUALS `not covered: ss 6(8) and 7(7) save only the decisions they describe; the Act does not say what follows otherwise`

§ `ss 5(3), (4), 6A(2) -- into the NSW Supreme Court`

-- Arguments: where, related case pending in NSW, view (i), view (ii), view (iii), then the four s 6A(1) facts.
-- A Queensland Supreme Court case related to one pending in NSW: s 5(3)(b)(i).
#ASSERT `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` `the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court, as it is related to a case pending there and more appropriately decided there (s 5(3)(b)(i) or (4)(b)(i))`
#ASSERT `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` `the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court, as more appropriately decided there having regard to where it could have been brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(ii) or (4)(b)(ii))`
#ASSERT `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` `the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(iii) or (4)(b)(iii))`
#ASSERT `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` `the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the court has not formed any of the views s 5(3) or (4) requires, so it is not required to transfer your case`
-- s 6A(2): a federal matter proceeding in the Federal Court; s 5(4) does not apply; the court may transfer.
#ASSERT `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` `the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `may: s 5(4) does not apply to your case (s 6A(2)), but the federal court may transfer it to the NSW Supreme Court if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice, including keeping related cases together`
#ASSERT `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` `the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `may: s 5(4) does not apply to your case (s 6A(2)), but the federal court may transfer it to the NSW Supreme Court if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice, including keeping related cases together`
#ASSERT `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` `the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 6A(2) -- s 5(4) does not apply, and the federal court does not consider a transfer appropriate`
-- s 6A(2) speaks only of the federal courts: the same facts in another Supreme Court still go under s 5(3).
#ASSERT `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` `the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court, as it is related to a case pending there and more appropriately decided there (s 5(3)(b)(i) or (4)(b)(i))`

§ `s 8 -- removal into the Supreme Court`

#ASSERT `Can my case be removed into the Supreme Court question` `a NSW court other than the Supreme Court` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court may, on your application or its own motion, remove your case into the Supreme Court, so that the related case could be brought to it (s 8(1)(b)(i)); it may later send it back (s 8(3))`
#ASSERT `Can my case be removed into the Supreme Court question` `a tribunal established by or under an Act` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court may, on your application or its own motion, remove your case into the Supreme Court so that a transfer to another court can be considered (s 8(1)(b)(ii)); it may later send it back (s 8(3))`
-- A related case pending, but no view that it could be transferred in: limb (i) fails.
#ASSERT `Can my case be removed into the Supreme Court question` `a NSW court other than the Supreme Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Supreme Court has not formed either view s 8(1)(b) requires`
#ASSERT `Can my case be removed into the Supreme Court question` `somewhere else` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 8 reaches only cases pending in a NSW court other than the Supreme Court, or in a tribunal established by or under an Act`

§ `s 10 -- consumer case sent down`

#ASSERT `Can my consumer case be sent to a lower court question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Can my consumer case be sent to a lower court question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Can my consumer case be sent to a lower court question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Can my consumer case be sent to a lower court question` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Can my consumer case be sent to a lower court question` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Can my consumer case be sent to a lower court question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE))

§ `s 7 -- where an appeal goes`

-- From a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court, no Schedule matter: s 7(3).
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court` `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: an appeal with no matter arising under a Schedule Act goes only to the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court` `the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an appeal with no matter arising under a Schedule Act may be brought only in the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court` `the High Court, with special leave` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: an appeal with no matter arising under a Schedule Act may be brought only in the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(3))`
-- With a Schedule matter: s 7(4).
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court` `the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act goes only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, as the case requires (s 7(4)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court` `the High Court, with special leave` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes, with special leave: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act may go to the High Court with its special leave (s 7(4)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court` `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act may be brought only in a federal Full Court or, with special leave, the High Court (s 7(4))`
-- From a single judge of a federal court: s 7(1), s 7(2).
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an appeal from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia cannot be brought in the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` `the other of the two federal courts` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an appeal cannot be brought from the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia to the other of those courts (s 7(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` `the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (any other)` `the other of the two federal courts` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: an appeal cannot be brought from the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia to the other of those courts (s 7(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (any other)` `the High Court, with special leave` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`
-- From a State Family Court: s 7(5) only with both kinds of matter, and it never names the court.
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a judge of a State Family Court` `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not decided: s 7(5) lets this State Family Court appeal be dealt with as if it raised no Schedule matter, but does not say which court hears it, and NSW has no State Family Court (s 3(2)), so the Act names no court for it`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a judge of a State Family Court` `the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not decided: s 7(5) lets this State Family Court appeal be dealt with as if it raised no Schedule matter, but does not say which court hears it, and NSW has no State Family Court (s 3(2)), so the Act names no court for it`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `a decision of a judge of a State Family Court` `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` `another decision` `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`

§ `s 7(6) -- an appeal begun in the Court of Appeal`

#ASSERT `Must my appeal move to a federal Full Court question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (s 7(6))`
#ASSERT `Must my appeal move to a federal Full Court question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the interests of justice require the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court to go on and decide your appeal (s 7(6)); its decision stands (s 7(7)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must my appeal move to a federal Full Court question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 7(6) applies only once the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court has begun hearing an appeal that raises a matter under a Schedule Act`
#ASSERT `Must my appeal move to a federal Full Court question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 7(6) applies only once the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court has begun hearing an appeal that raises a matter under a Schedule Act`

§ `s 11(1) -- the law applied`

-- A NSW road claim transferred to the Victorian Supreme Court: the law of Victoria, where it sits, including its choice of law rules.
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the law of the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules used in a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
-- A claim under a NSW statute heard in Victoria: the NSW written and unwritten law.
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law your right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules used in a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: s 11 applies only where the court is, or is likely to be, exercising cross-vested jurisdiction`

§ `ss 11(3), 12 -- after the transfer`

#ASSERT `What carries over after the transfer question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the steps already taken, and orders made, in the old court count as taken in the new court, unless the new court orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the new court may order costs of the earlier stage, as they have not been dealt with (s 12)`
#ASSERT `What carries over after the transfer question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the steps already taken, and orders made, in the old court count as taken in the new court, unless the new court orders otherwise (s 11(3)); s 12 gives no power over costs of the earlier stage already dealt with by another court`
#ASSERT `What carries over after the transfer question` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: ss 11(3) and 12 apply only once a case has been transferred or removed`

§ `s 13 -- no appeal`

#ASSERT `Can I appeal the transfer decision question` `a decision about transferring or removing the case under this Act` EQUALS `no: no appeal lies from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal the transfer decision question` `a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)` EQUALS `no: no appeal lies from a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1) (s 13(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal the transfer decision question` `another decision` EQUALS `not covered: s 13 bars appeals only from transfer and removal decisions and s 11(1) evidence and procedure decisions`

§ `s 5(8) -- the lawyer`

#ASSERT `Can my lawyer act for me in the new court question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, in your transferred case, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can my lawyer act for me in the new court question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, in your related case decided together with the transferred one, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can my lawyer act for me in the new court question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 5(8) gives the right only to a lawyer entitled to practise in the court the case was transferred from, for the transferred case and related cases decided with it`
#ASSERT `Can my lawyer act for me in the new court question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 5(8) gives the right only to a lawyer entitled to practise in the court the case was transferred from, for the transferred case and related cases decided with it`

§ `s 14(2) -- enforcement`

#ASSERT `Can my judgment be enforced in NSW question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: a NSW Supreme Court judgment given wholly or partly in cross-vested jurisdiction is enforceable in NSW as if given in the Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
#ASSERT `Can my judgment be enforced in NSW question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 14(2) speaks only of NSW Supreme Court judgments given wholly or partly in cross-vested jurisdiction`
#ASSERT `Can my judgment be enforced in NSW question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: s 14(2) speaks only of NSW Supreme Court judgments given wholly or partly in cross-vested jurisdiction`
